Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 00:10:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] REPA: representation alignment loss for DiT training
 (Qwen-Image-2.1)

Adds REPA (Yu et al., ICLR 2025, github.com/sihyun-yu/REPA) as an optional
auxiliary loss. A small trainable projector maps one early block's image
tokens onto the patch features a frozen DINOv2 produces for the clean
training image; the negative cosine similarity, averaged over tokens, is
added to the denoising loss. Off unless the process config has a `repa:`
block (toolkit/config_modules.py RepaConfig).

- toolkit/repa.py: RepaRegularizer and the pure helpers. The encoder is fed
  the exact pre-VAE pixels (the existing require_pixel_tensor_cache flag, as
  MiniMax H3 uses), at the token grid's aspect ratio so each DiT token is
  compared with the encoder patches over the same pixels. With cached
  latents the encoder features are computed once, stored beside the latent
  cache and the encoder is freed; without, it stays resident.
- The block output is captured by a forward hook registered lazily on the
  first primary prediction (so it sits on a block_compile wrapper). Only the
  primary prediction captures. It fails with a message when nothing was
  captured, the block ran twice, nothing trainable is upstream of it, or the
  setup cannot work (unsupported arch, guided loss, mean_flow, video, ...).
- The projector is its own optimizer group, initialised as in the paper from
  its own RNG so a REPA run and its control share noise and timesteps, and
  saved as repa_projector.pt beside optimizer.pt (outside the LoRA, the Hub
  push and checkpoint cleanup) so a resume continues with it.
- BaseModel gains get_repa_token_grid / get_repa_target_tokens /
  get_repa_hidden_size / get_repa_blocks; Qwen-Image-2.1 implements the first
  two. SDTrainer gets five small integration points.

