 Everything else should work the same including layer targeting.
 
 
+## REPA (representation alignment)
+
+[REPA](https://github.com/sihyun-yu/REPA) (Yu et al., *Representation Alignment for Generation: Training Diffusion
+Transformers Is Easier Than You Think*, ICLR 2025) is an optional extra loss for transformer models. A small trainable
+projector maps the image tokens of one early transformer block onto the patch features a frozen vision encoder
+(DINOv2) produces for the clean training image, and the negative cosine similarity between the two, averaged over
+tokens, is added to the denoising loss:
+
+```
+loss = denoise_loss + weight * mean_tokens(-cos(proj(h_block), encoder(x)))
+```
+
+It is off unless the process config has a `repa` block, next to `train`, `model` and `datasets`. These are the defaults:
+
+```yaml
+    repa:
+      enabled: true                    # true when the block is present; false switches it off without deleting it
+      weight: 0.5                      # lambda on the alignment term
+      block_index: 8                   # 0-indexed: the output of this block of the model's block list
+      encoder: facebook/dinov2-giant   # a Hugging Face ViT whose last_hidden_state ends with the patch grid
+      encoder_resolution: 518          # the pixel side the encoder is aimed at, see below
+      projector: mlp                   # mlp (the paper's: Linear-SiLU-Linear-SiLU-Linear) or linear (LayerNorm + Linear)
+      projector_dim: 2048              # hidden width of the mlp projector
+```
+
+Supported models: Qwen-Image-2.1. The values above are SimpleTuner's Qwen-Image-2.1 preset (DINOv2 ViT-g/14,
+block 8, lambda 0.5) with the paper's projector. Other models are added with two methods on their model class
+(`get_repa_token_grid` and `get_repa_target_tokens` in `toolkit/models/base_model.py`) and a
+`get_transformer_block_names` entry. A model without them fails at setup with a message saying so.
+
+`block_index` counts from 0 and aligns the output of that block, as SimpleTuner does. The paper's `encoder_depth=8`
+is the output of the 8th block, which is `block_index: 7`.
+
+### What it does when you train
+
+- **Pixels.** REPA turns on the same switch MiniMax H3 uses (`cache_tensors_to_disk`, forced on for the datasets that
+  cache latents), so the exact image each latent was encoded from is stored in the latent cache and handed to the
+  encoder. There is no VAE decode. A latent cache made without it is encoded once more on the first REPA run.
+- **Alignment is token for token.** The encoder sees the image at the aspect ratio of the token grid, resized so its
+  patch grid is a whole multiple of it (a 768 px Qwen-Image-2.1 image is 48 x 48 tokens and goes to the encoder at
+  672 px, one patch per token), and the encoder features are average-pooled onto the token grid. The encoder never
+  sees more than twice `encoder_resolution` on a side; larger images are encoded at that size and the features
+  are interpolated onto the token grid.
+- **With cached latents (`cache_latents_to_disk`, the usual case).** Before training, one pass computes the encoder
+  features of every image, regularization sets included, and stores them as fp16 beside the latent cache
+  (`_repa_cache`, about 7 MB per 768 px image and 12.6 MB per 1024 px image with ViT-g, so 100 images take about
+  0.7 GB). Then the encoder is freed. A later run on the same data finds them and does not load the encoder at all.
+  Delete the folder to reclaim the space. The features are keyed by the latent cache entry, the encoder and
+  `encoder_resolution`, so changing any of them makes new files. With `cache_latents` alone the features stay in memory.
+- **Without cached latents.** The encoder stays on the GPU and runs on every step, on the pixels of the batch: about
+  4.5 GB for `dinov2-giant`, 1.2 GB for `dinov2-large` and 0.35 GB for `dinov2-base`. Use `dinov2-base` on small
+  cards. Whatever augmentation the dataset applies is what the encoder sees.
+- **The projector** is a parameter group of its own at `train.lr`, initialised as in the paper. It exists for
+  training only and never enters the LoRA. It is saved as `repa_projector.pt` beside `optimizer.pt` and restored
+  when you resume, and it is skipped by the Hugging Face push and by old-checkpoint cleanup. A resumed run with a
+  different encoder, block or projector size starts the projector from scratch and says so.
+- **Turning REPA on or off between a save and a resume** changes the optimizer's parameter groups, so `optimizer.pt`
+  cannot be loaded and the optimizer starts fresh. REPA prints a warning when it sees this.
+- **Reproducibility.** The projector is initialised from `training_seed` (0 if unset) and REPA does not touch the
+  global random streams, so a run with REPA and a run without it that share a `training_seed` draw the same noise,
+  timesteps and data order.
+- **Logs.** `loss/repa` (weighted), `repa/cos` (the mean cosine similarity, 1 is perfect alignment) and `loss/denoise`
+  go to the usual loggers at `logging.log_every`. The total loss goes negative as alignment improves, so compare runs
+  on `loss/denoise`.
+
+Only the primary prediction is aligned. REPA fails with an explanation at setup, or on the first step, rather than
+doing nothing: for an unsupported model, `train_unet: false`, a trainable set that does not reach the block, a block
+that runs twice in one prediction, and the loss paths that bypass the single prediction (guided loss,
+`loss_type: mean_flow`, `unconditional_path`, `short_and_long_captions`, video and audio datasets).
+
+| Feature | With REPA |
+|---|---|
+| Gradient checkpointing | supported |
+| `block_compile` | supported: the capture hook sits on the compiled block's wrapper, outside the compiled graph |
+| Whole-model `compile` | not verified |
+| Quantization, `low_vram`, layer offloading | supported |
+| Multiple GPUs (accelerate) | supported: accelerate prepares the projector and features are cached on the main process |
+| Regularization datasets (`is_reg`, `reg_weight`) | aligned like the others and scaled by `reg_weight` with the rest of the loss |
+| Differential output preservation, blank prompt preservation | supported: their extra predictions are not aligned |
+| Gradient accumulation, batch size above 1, buckets, flips, `num_repeats` | supported |
+| Reference images (Qwen-Image-2.1 edit-style datasets) | supported: only the target image's tokens are aligned |
+| Masks (`mask_path`, alpha masks) | masks weight the denoising loss only, REPA is not masked |
+
+REPA is experimental. `repa/cos` rising over a run shows the projector learning to match the encoder.
+
+
